<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kolkhoz</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as kolkhozy, collective farm, kolkhoznik</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A kolkhoz was a collective farm in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, the main form of Soviet agriculture alongside the state farms, or sovkhozes. The first were organised soon after 1918, and after forced collectivisation the agricultural artel became the only form by 1938. Legally a cooperative, in practice it was run by district and provincial authorities, and membership was neither voluntary nor freely left. Members, kolkhozniki, were paid by labour days, often in little or no cash, while the farm sold its produce to the state at very low fixed prices, a major source of state income. Private plots of about one acre produced a large share of output. Their number declined rapidly after 1992.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kolkhoz.jpg" alt="Kolkhoz" width="330" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Неизвестный художник (не указан в выходных данных плаката; отсутствует авторская монограмма или подпись) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Колхозник,_читай_книгу!_Книга_поможет_выполнить_план_второй_большевистской_весны.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
